#!/usr/bin/env bash
# Prints the top of a release's description: how to install this version.
# The release workflow puts it above the list of changes GitHub writes.
#
# Usage: scripts/release-notes <version>     e.g. scripts/release-notes v0.1.0
set -euo pipefail

version=${1:?usage: scripts/release-notes <version>}
repo=${PAIL_REPO:-chrisdmacrae/pail}
base="https://github.com/$repo/releases/download/$version"

cat <<NOTES
## Install

### The server, on Proxmox

On the Proxmox host, as root. This makes a container and sets Pail up in it, with storage and everything builds, functions and containers need.

\`\`\`bash
PAIL_VERSION=$version bash -c "\$(curl -fsSL $base/pail-proxmox.sh)"
\`\`\`

Already running Pail from this installer? Update it in place, with the container's ID in place of \`101\`:

\`\`\`bash
pct exec 101 -- pail-update $version
\`\`\`

On another kind of Linux machine, \`pail-server_linux_amd64.tar.gz\` and \`pail-server_linux_arm64.tar.gz\` below hold the server by itself. The [README](https://github.com/$repo#readme) says what it needs around it.

### The command line, with Homebrew

On macOS or Linux:

\`\`\`bash
brew install chrisdmacrae/tap/pail
\`\`\`

To move an earlier install to this version:

\`\`\`bash
brew upgrade pail
\`\`\`

Without Homebrew, or on Windows, download \`pail\` for your system from the files below and put it on your \`PATH\`.

Then point it at your Pail once, and deploy a folder:

\`\`\`bash
pail login https://pail.lan
pail up ./dist
\`\`\`
NOTES
